# tools/ales_stereo_envelope_witness.rish — stereo_envelope: the envelope follower carried into stereo, one linked detector for the time-varying family to come (ALES160).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES160.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-064459_fill-ales160-lotus-stereo-envelope.md
# Stands on: lotus/envelope.rye (ALES52 — the mono envelope follower, its env_step, coeff_ok, and EnvelopeError reused whole for the forced-unit-step exact-reach guarantee, the attack/release asymmetry, and the per-sample
# proof that both channels equal the mono follow of the linked key), lotus/stereo_gate.rye (ALES156 — the linked-decision law whose linked key this shares), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip and its balance invariant),
# lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, sample_max).
#
# lotus/stereo_envelope.rye follows a span of sample-PAIRS in a master by an attack and a release fraction, advancing ONE shared envelope toward the linked peak max(|left|, |right|) clamped to the rail and writing the shared
# value into BOTH channels — one linked detector, the time base the whole stereo time-varying dynamics family waits for. The detector is shared: the louder channel decides the shared loudness (following each channel by its
# own magnitude would pump the image), and the one curve is written to both so left == right after — the detector aligned across the field. EnvelopeError (BadCoeff, BadRange) reused whole; the linked detector adds no fault.
# Values only, never a length. The crux is the linked detector law (the shared curve equal to the mono follow of the per-sample linked key byte for byte, both channels equal to it, the independent per-channel follow
# differing), the balance law (left == right after, length held), the instantaneous law (unit attack and release the linked peak every sample), the convergence laws (a constant reached exactly and monotonically from below
# and above, a faster attack no slower), the carry law (a span followed in two pieces equal to the whole once on both), and the silence/atomicity/degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_envelope_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-envelope: Language EN — stereo_envelope follows a span of sample-pairs in a stereo master by an attack and a release fraction, advancing one shared envelope toward the linked peak (the max of the two channels' magnitudes, clamped to the rail) and writing the shared value into both channels. The detector is shared: the louder channel decides the shared loudness each sample, and the one curve is written to both so the left and right leave equal, the detector aligned in time across the stereo field. It validates the one attack, the one release, and the span once against the shared length before any write, so a refusal never follows one channel and leaves the other torn. It writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced; an all-silent master follows to zero on both and an empty span is a clean no-op on both."
say "ales-stereo-envelope: Style Radiant — ALES52 named the mono envelope follower: a running estimate of how loud a signal is right now, rising quickly toward a new peak (the attack) and falling slowly after it (the release), rendered as a magnitude curve so the attack/release limiter, compressor, gate, and expander all read one proven time base. The stereo memoryless quartet — gate, compressor, limiter, expander — each act on a pair the instant they see it, with no memory of the sound a moment ago. This is the crux that opens time-varying stereo dynamics, and it must be linked. One truth: the detector is shared. The key is the linked peak, the louder channel deciding the shared loudness exactly as the stereo gate, compressor, and expander read their per-pair key; following each channel by its own magnitude would give two envelopes that lean in and ease off on different samples, and a later gain read from two curves would pump the image. So the one curve is written to both channels — left equal to right after, the detector aligned across the field, one time base a stereo dynamics rung reads once. The crux is six laws: the linked detector law (the shared curve equal to the mono follow of the per-sample linked key byte for byte, both channels equal to it, the independent per-channel follow genuinely differing); the balance law (left equal to right after, the lengths held); the instantaneous law (unit attack and unit release making the shared envelope the linked peak every sample); the convergence laws (a constant linked input reached exactly and monotonically from below via the attack and from above via the release, never overshooting, a faster attack rising at least as fast); the carry law (a span followed in two pieces equal to following the whole once, byte for byte on both); and the silence, atomicity, and degenerate law (an all-silent master following to zero on both, an illegal coefficient and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-envelope: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) carrying one shared i64 envelope, one comparison for the linked key and at most one multiply-then-divide per sample (ALES52's proven env_step reused whole), the envelope bounded in the range zero to sample_max which fits i16, so nothing on the path can overflow; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a follower writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit, the shared value asserted identical on both channels sample by sample); the attack and release are validated once by the one coeff_ok rule ALES52 proves and the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never follows one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoEnvelopeError is ALES52's EnvelopeError (BadCoeff, BadRange) reused whole, the linked detector adding no fault. The linked detector law is proven against the mono follow of a synthetic per-sample linked-key clip — both channels equal to it byte for byte and equal to each other, while the independent per-channel follow of the right alone is shown to genuinely differ; the instantaneous law proves unit attack and release make both channels the per-sample max magnitude (including the full-scale negative peak clamped to the rail); the convergence laws prove a steady left peak of 8000 reached exactly and monotonically from silence and a 10000-then-2000 linked peak eased down to exactly 2000, both channels tracking the one curve; the faster-attack law proves 1:2 no slower than 1:8 at every sample; the carry law proves a span followed in two pieces equal to the whole once on both channels; and the silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying zero, the length held, each of the four refusals (a bad attack, a bad release, a zero denominator, an out-of-range span) leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the attack and release fractions per sample index rather than milliseconds against a clock, the curve not yet routed into a gain (the next rung), no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_envelope"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_envelope.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-envelope: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_envelope.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-envelope: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-envelope: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-envelope' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-envelope: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-envelope line"
